    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">COVID-19, mild (U07.1)</content>. Mild illness on day two: no breathlessness, a normal oxygen saturation and clear lungs. Age over 50 raises the risk of severe disease, and the risk rises steeply after 65; he has no other risk condition and is vaccinated. Nirmatrelvir with ritonavir was offered: ritonavir changes how many drugs are handled, and with an opium paste of unknown strength the effect on him cannot be predicted closely; omeprazole is not a problem. After discussion he declined, preferring to watch and return if worse. Remdesivir infusions were not wanted. [S]<list><item>Rest, fluids and acetaminophen as needed, no more than 3 g a day.</item><item>Honey in warm water for the cough; no codeine or dextromethorphan cough syrup, sedating antihistamine or sleep aid while he takes the remedy.</item><item>Stay away from others until fever-free for 24 hours without medicine and improving, then mask and keep distance for five more days; work from his quarters.</item><item>Call 911 for breathlessness, chest pain, confusion or blue lips; return for oxygen saturation below 94% on the home oximeter.</item><item>Review at the preventive visit in three weeks.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Opium-containing remedy during an acute illness (F11.90)</content>. Illness, poor sleep and cough can tempt a larger dose, and fever can make an opioid&apos;s sedation harder to judge. He is alert, his pupils and breathing are normal, and he has taken no more than usual. [S]<list><item>No more than his usual amount, and none on a night he feels drowsy.</item><item>Naloxone at his bedside; a colleague in his quarters knows where it is and how to use it.</item><item>No alcohol while unwell.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Reflux esophagitis on omeprazole (K21.00)</content>. Quiet; coughing can bring on heartburn. [S]<list><item>Continue omeprazole each morning to complete eight weeks.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: CDC Clinical Practice Guideline for Prescribing Opioids for Pain, United States, 2022 (2022); Preventing Spread of Respiratory Viruses When You&apos;re Sick (2024); COVID-19 Treatment: Clinical Care for Outpatients (2025); IDSA Guidelines on the Treatment and Management of Patients with COVID-19 (living guideline)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Preventive visit in three weeks as booked; return sooner for breathlessness, chest pain, a low oxygen reading, or fever beyond five days. Next scheduled visit 2026-07-28: periodic adult preventive visit. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
